From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 19:57:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: syscon: don't attach the parent clock of the Qualcomm
 TCSR

of_syscon_register() takes the first "clocks" entry of a syscon node as
its register clock and keeps it prepared. For a node that is also a
clock provider, such as the Qualcomm Glymur and X1E TCSR, that entry is
the provider's parent (the RPMh CXO clock), not a register clock.

On Glymur, the SCM node's qcom,dload-mode looks up the TCSR syscon, so
CXO stays prepared, and its RPMh vote keeps the XO crystal on and
blocks AOSS sleep in system suspend.

Skip the clock lookup for the Glymur and X1E TCSR only: some clock
providers, such as the i.MX95 VPU CSR, do need their clock input for
register access.

Link: https://github.com/justririll/x1e-sleep
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/mfd/syscon.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/syscon.c b/drivers/mfd/syscon.c
index 21a7fcdd2737..ec238752485c 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/syscon.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/syscon.c
@@ -37,6 +37,16 @@ static const struct regmap_config syscon_regmap_config = {
 	.reg_stride = 4,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Clock controllers whose "clocks" are their parents, not a register clock.
+ * Keeping such a clock prepared from here pins it, e.g. an RPMh XO vote.
+ */
+static const struct of_device_id syscon_parent_clk_only[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "qcom,glymur-tcsr" },
+	{ .compatible = "qcom,x1e80100-tcsr" },
+	{ }
+};
+
 static struct syscon *of_syscon_register(struct device_node *np, bool check_res)
 {
 	struct clk *clk;
@@ -123,7 +133,10 @@ static struct syscon *of_syscon_register(struct device_node *np, bool check_res)
 	}
 
 	if (check_res) {
-		clk = of_clk_get(np, 0);
+		if (of_match_node(syscon_parent_clk_only, np))
+			clk = ERR_PTR(-ENOENT);
+		else
+			clk = of_clk_get(np, 0);
 		if (IS_ERR(clk)) {
 			ret = PTR_ERR(clk);
 			/* clock is optional */
